From 950934d3b2dc7dcc69b74f45f51e98dd0769b2e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:27:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] connman: a connection's own nameservers, also with DHCP

The nameserver fields of a connection refused any change while its IPv4
method was DHCP ("Not allowed"), although ConnMan uses
Nameservers.Configuration whatever the method is: with DHCP, the only
way to use other nameservers was a manual IPv4 address. Providers that
block names by DNS make this matter.

- The fields hold the connection's own nameservers
  (Nameservers.Configuration) and can be set with any method; with the
  three empty, the connection uses the nameservers the network gives.
  The fields used to be filled with the nameservers in use, so a save of
  a DHCP connection, even without a change, turned the network's
  nameservers into a fixed configuration, which then went with the
  connection to another network (the wired connection keeps its name
  from one network to the next).
- A field takes an IPv4 address (Kodi's number pad, as before), an IPv6
  address (the keyboard), or is cleared, from a short choice; an
  address is kept in its short form. 0.0.0.0 still clears the field.
- A row "In use now" shows the nameservers that the connection uses.
- A connection switched from DHCP to a manual IPv4 address without
  nameservers of its own keeps the ones it was using (those of the lease
  go away with it, and ConnMan's fallback ones do not resolve the names
  of the local network); nameservers the user cleared are not brought
  back.
- The list fields are emptied when a connection is opened: the dialog's
  structure is shared, and a list that the connection does not have
  kept the values of the connection opened before, which a save then
  stored on this one (nameservers and domains alike).
- A list of which no nameserver can be used with the connection's IPv4
  and IPv6 settings asks before it is saved: with nameservers of its
  own, ConnMan does not fall back on others, and names would not
  resolve.

Domains and timeservers keep their behaviour.

The idea of nameservers with DHCP comes from damnms (CoreELEC settings
add-on).
